WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:07.327
Nutritional grouping means feeding cows with similar requirements together, each group with its own ration.

2
00:00:07.377 --> 00:00:09.273
It is known to save feed cost.

3
00:00:09.323 --> 00:00:11.281
Can it also cut enteric methane?

4
00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:27.552
To find out, this study builds an open-source linear program that formulates diets from the ten ingredients already on the farm, for six hundred seventy-five cows in the University of Wisconsin's Arlington herd.

5
00:00:27.952 --> 00:00:32.743
It can minimize feed cost, minimize methane, or minimize both together.

6
00:00:32.793 --> 00:00:42.020
Constraints hold intake and energy within one percent of target, and keep protein, fiber, starch, fat and silage within guideline ranges.

7
00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:43.902
Milk yield is held constant.

8
00:00:45.552 --> 00:00:46.799
Start with one group.

9
00:00:46.849 --> 00:00:53.993
The farm's base diet costs seven dollars ninety-eight per cow per day, and emits four hundred eighty-six grams of methane.

10
00:00:54.393 --> 00:00:59.709
Minimize cost, and the ration drops to five forty-six, thirty-two percent cheaper.

11
00:00:59.759 --> 00:01:03.070
But methane rises to five hundred sixty-five grams.

12
00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:11.410
Minimize methane instead, and it falls twelve percent, to four hundred twenty-nine grams, while cost climbs to eight seventy-two.

13
00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:14.174
The dual objective lands in between:

14
00:01:14.224 --> 00:01:19.461
nearly the same low cost, with methane fifty-five grams below the cost-only diet.

15
00:01:21.111 --> 00:01:26.001
Now split the herd in two, by milk yield, days in milk, or energy requirement.

16
00:01:26.051 --> 00:01:28.189
The targeted outcome barely moves.

17
00:01:28.239 --> 00:01:30.128
What improves is the other one.

18
00:01:30.528 --> 00:01:34.978
When minimizing cost, grouping trims methane by about thirty grams.

19
00:01:35.028 --> 00:01:40.538
When minimizing methane, it saves thirty-six to seventy-four cents per cow per day.

20
00:01:40.588 --> 00:01:45.304
Grouped by energy, the low-methane diet costs the same as the base diet.

21
00:01:45.704 --> 00:01:47.446
Why so little on the target?

22
00:01:47.496 --> 00:01:52.697
Fat and fiber, the nutrients that drive methane, had the same limits in every group.

23
00:01:52.747 --> 00:01:55.724
So the single-group diet was already at the edge.

24
00:01:57.824 --> 00:02:00.775
The rations themselves change only modestly.

25
00:02:00.825 --> 00:02:04.777
Cheaper diets lean on soybean hulls and high-moisture corn.

26
00:02:04.827 --> 00:02:13.048
Low-methane diets swap in dry corn and whole cottonseed, for more starch and fat, and crude protein rises to twenty percent.

27
00:02:15.648 --> 00:02:16.197
Caveats:

28
00:02:16.247 --> 00:02:22.788
milk is assumed not to respond, protein is a simple proxy, and it is one herd with ten ingredients.

29
00:02:25.188 --> 00:02:25.990
The takeaway:

30
00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:27.633
optimize the ration first.

31
00:02:27.683 --> 00:02:32.199
Grouping then refines it, and mostly helps the goal you were not aiming at.
